EDT recommends abstain, CDT recommends sending. Need explain. David wants Bathsheba, fears revolt. Charismatic kings act justly and low revolt; uncharismatic act unjustly and high revolt. Unjust acts don't cause revolt; charisma causes both. David doesn't know charisma. If summoning is unjust, choosing summon is evidence uncharismatic and revolt; EDT abstains. But CDT: do(summon) does not change charisma, and unjust act not cause revolt; if he wants Bathsheba enough, expected causal utility of summoning high; charisma fixed. Need mention.\n\nNeed also discuss when CDT = EDT: if action probabilistically independent of dependency hypotheses / states relevant to outcome under intervention, or Gibbard-Harper axioms statistical independence of A and A→O_j yields equality. Source says if accept two axioms then statistical independence suffices. Need explain: when no common causes connecting action and outcomes except through action, conditional probability equals counterfactual probability. In randomized experiments, do(a) distribution equals P(o|a). In confounded cases, differ.\n\nNeed discuss Lewis's result: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability, triviality. We can mention but not deep.\n\nNeed explain \"if A necessarily implies O_j\" from source maybe ambiguous; use counterfactual \"if A were performed, O_j would obtain\". Need maybe note source's phrase \"necessarily implies\" should not be read as logical necessity but as counterfactual dependence in closest worlds/intervention.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical example with table. Apple: states good/bad, prior p. Actions eat apple/eat orange? Define utilities. CDT: U(apple)=p u(happy)+(1-p)u(sad) if eating apple's causal effects conditional on state. If orange known causes sad? Need maybe simple.\n\nNeed maybe present an algorithmic recipe:\n1. Specify actions, outcomes, utility.\n2. Specify causal model or dependency hypotheses (DAG/SCM), including variables for states, predictions, traits.\n3. For each action, compute interventional distribution P(o | do(a)), not P(o | a), by cutting arrows into action, using adjustment or simulation.\n4. Compute expected utility and choose max.\n5. For policies, evaluate do(policy) and choose max. If model uncertainty, average over models with prior (or updated but not action-conditioned? Need discuss).\nNeed mention how to handle observations: if action is chosen after evidence e, use conditional interventional distribution P(o | do(a), e) or posterior over exogenous variables given e. That's important for applying to unfamiliar cases. Need be precise: after observing evidence E=e not caused by action, update beliefs about exogenous variables U; then evaluate do(a) in posterior SCM. If evidence is caused by action or downstream, careful not to double count? In decision at time t, condition on information available up to t, then intervene on future action. Formula: V(a | e)=Σ_o u(o) P(o | do(a), e) where e is pre-action evidence. In SCM, update posterior over exogenous noise given e, then set A=a. This is Pearl's effect of causes on effects given evidence. Need include.\n\nNeed discuss policies with feedback: If policy uses observations, evaluating policy π uses intervention on action variables as functions of observations; not simply one-shot do(A=a). Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe distinguish claims: Source claims CDT recommends action causing best expected outcome, formula, difference from EDT, Lewis, David, Newcomb, criticisms, vagueness. My additions: SCM/do-calculus operationalization, adjustment formula, policy evaluation, decision recipe, some normative comments. Need explicitly flag.\n\nNeed consider target word count. Need maybe 2500 words. Let's plan final essay.\n\nPotential title: \"Causal Decision Theory: Expected Utility Under Intervention\".\n\nStructure:\n1. Introduction (200 words)\n2. Expected utility and two probability kernels (350 words)\n   - EDT formula, CDT formula, explanation.\n3. Counterfactuals and interventions (450 words)\n   - imaging and do-operator. Proposed operational interpretation.\n4. How to apply CDT: a recipe (450 words)\n   - variables, causal graph, intervention, adjustment, uncertainty, policies.\n5. Examples (600 words)\n   - Apple/orange (simple), David/Bathsheba (common cause), Newcomb (prediction), maybe Adversarial Offer or psychopath button briefly.\n6. Dependencies, counterfactuals, and when CDT=EDT (250 words)\n7. Open problems/criticisms (300 words)\n   - vagueness, Egan, Death in Damascus, Dutch books, ratifiability. My operational interpretation limitations.\n8. Conclusion (100)\nReferences (150?) Total maybe 2900. Need keep not too long. Could be 2600. Need include references to source and primary works. Need not lengthy bibliography. Maybe references list with 6-8 items.\n\nNeed maybe mention mathematical notation:\nLet A be action variable with values a. Let O outcome variable, U utility. EDT:\nV_EDT(a)=E[U | A=a] = Σ_o u(o)P(O=o | A=a).\nCDT:\nV_CDT(a)=Σ_o u(o)P(O=o □→ A=a) or P(a □→ o). Use box arrow.\nIn SCM M=(U,V,F,P(U)): do(A=a) yields M_a. P_a(o)=P_{M_a}(O=o). Then V_CDT(a)=E_{M_a}[u(O)]. If observed evidence e prior to action: update P(u|e), then V(a|e)=Σ_u P(u|e) u(O_{M_a}(u)). Or equivalently P(o | do(a), e).\nAdjustment: If Z backdoor, P(o | do(a)) = Σ_z P(o | a,z)P(z). If front-door etc. If no confounding, P(o | do(a))=P(o|a).\nPolicy: π: X→A. Evaluate V(π)=E_{M_{do(π)}}[u(O)], where do(π) sets A_t=π_t(H_t). If deterministic, can be represented as intervention on action nodes.\n\nNeed maybe discuss dependency hypotheses H. Source/Egan: CDT uses unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses. Formal: V_CDT(a)=Σ_h P(h) Σ_o u(o)P_h(o|do(a),h). But if action is evidence about h (e.g. predictor predicts action), EDT would condition P(h|a); CDT does not. But after observing evidence not caused by action, update P(h|e). Need mention.\n\nNeed perhaps clarify \"causes best outcome in expectation\" doesn't mean actual g