nces not too lengthy. Need maybe Wikipedia source as reference. But target includes references. Could list 8.\n\nNeed ensure target words. Let's write. Need maybe use LaTeX inline. Need be readable.\n\nPotential details for mathematical operationalization:\n\nSCM: M=(\\mathbf{U},\\mathbf{V},F,P(\\mathbf{U})). Each V_i=F_i(PA_i,U_i). Action A variable. Intervention do(A=a) creates M_a with F_A replaced by constant a. Counterfactual outcome O_a(u)=O_{M_a}(u). Then V_CDT(a)=∑_u P(u)D(O_a(u)). If O discrete. If continuous integral. If observed evidence e: posterior P(u|e) and V(a|e)=∑_u P(u|e)D(O_a(u)). This is precise.\nNeed mention if action is impossible in some worlds? do handles if allowed; if impossible maybe undefined. Source imaging assumes A possible.\n\nDependency hypotheses H: each h is an SCM or causal graph. V(a)=∑_h P(h) V_h(a). Egan's criticism: using prior P(h) can be bad if action provides evidence about h. But CDT says action is not evidence about causal structure for purposes of causal expected utility. We can propose: use posterior P(h|e) from admissible evidence e, but not P(h|a). If the agent's act is predicted by h, there is tension. Need distinguish.\n\nBackdoor formula: If graph has confounder Z affecting A and O, then P(o|do(a))=∑_z P(o|a,z)P(z). Need maybe example: David charisma Z? If A=summon, O=revolt, Z=charisma. Since charisma affects A tendency and revolt, but action not cause revolt? To compute causal effect of summoning on revolt, if charisma is confounder and we have data, adjust: P(revolt|do(summon))=∑_c P(revolt|summon,c)P(c). But if summoning itself doesn't cause revolt, conditional P(revolt|summon,c) maybe independent of summoning? Then result is baseline revolt probability for charisma distribution. EDT uses P(revolt|summon) which overweights uncharismatic. Need nice.\n\nNeed maybe include dominance principle: If one action causally dominates (better in every state under intervention), CDT chooses it. Newcomb two-box dominates because contents fixed relative to intervention. Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe clarify actions vs policies: If evaluating policy in Newcomb-like predictor that predicts policy, one-shot CDT may still two-box if prediction fixed before implementation. If predictor can predict policy and you choose policy before predictor? Hmm. Need not.\n\nPotential risk: user asks \"Explain how the theory evaluates alternative actions or policies and how it treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Where the source leaves an important question open, explain uncertainty and, if you think a reasonable operational interpretation is available, develop and justify it. Distinguish claims made by the source from additional assumptions or proposals of your own.\" Need explicit distinction. I can have paragraphs: \"The source claims...\"; \"The source does not specify... I will use...\". Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"target approximately 2,400–3,000 words\". Need ensure final length. Let's approximate as I write. Use concise but detailed.\n\nLet's begin drafting final.\n\nTitle: Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by the Expected Consequences of Interventions\n\nIntro maybe 170 words.\n\nSection 1: The basic rule.\n\nMathematical definitions. Need maybe use D for desirability. Need mention expected utility.\n\nPotential text:\n\"Let \\mathcal{A} be finite set of acts, \\mathcal{O} outcomes, D:\\mathcal{O}\\to\\mathbb{R}. EDT uses conditional probability: V_EDT(A)=∑_j P(O_j|A)D(O_j). CDT, following Gibbard and Harper, uses V_CDT(A)=∑_j P(A \\square\\to O_j)D(O_j).\" Need use source arrow maybe A \\to O_j but explain counterfactual. Need maybe note \"the arrow must not be read as material implication.\" Good.\n\nNeed discuss Lewis: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability. If they were equal, CDT=EDT. Source.\n\nSection 2: Interpretations of counterfactual probabilities. Imaging. SCM. Need write:\n\"The reference notes CDT does not specify algorithm. Lewis proposed imaging: P(A □→ O)=∑_w P(w)1_O(w_A) ... This can be stochastic. But Shaffer's regress... Source. I propose using causal models.\" Need maybe cite Pearl as additional.\n\nSCM formula. Need write in detail.\n\nSection 3: How to decide. Recipe with subheadings.\nNeed include:\n- ex ante vs after evidence.\n- model uncertainty.\n- policies.\n\nSection 4: Examples.\nUse equations.\n\nApple example:\n\"Suppose prior p apple good. If eat apple causes happiness H if good, sadness S if bad; orange causes sadness S_o. V(apple)=pD(H)+(1-p)D(S). V(orange)=D(S_o). If V(apple)>V(orange), eat apple. This is just expected utility, because no confounding. If apple's quality is a common cause? Not relevant.\" Good.\n\nDavid example with causal graph. Could include equation:\nLet C∈{ch, un}. P(C=ch)=q. Revolt probabilities r_ch, r_un with r_ch<r_un. Utility of summoning includes pleasure b and revolt cost c. CDT:\nV_CDT(summon)=q[D(success? maybe no revolt)??]. Need simpler: Because A does not causally affect C and A does not cause revolt except perhaps through? Then P(revolt | do(summon)) = q r_ch + (1-q) r_un (if no causal effect of unjust act). EDT: P(revolt|summon)=P(C=un|summon)r_un + P(C=ch|summon)r_ch, and since uncharismatic more likely to summon, P(C=un|summon)>P(C=un), making revolt more likely. CDT may summon if benefit outweighs baseline risk; EDT abstain if summoning is too strong evidence of uncharisma. Good. Need note source says CDT recommends sending for her. We can assume utility.\n\nNewcomb:\nLet q=P(predict one). Under do(one): contents fixed. V_CDT(one)=q*1,000,000+(1-q)*0. V_CDT(two)=q*1,001,000+(1-q)*1,000 = V_CDT(one)+1,000. Therefore two. EDT: V_EDT(one)≈P(predict one|one)*1M ≈ .99M; V_EDT(two)≈P(predict one|two)*1M? Actually if two, box B empty plus 1000, so ≈ .01*1M? Need if accuracy 99: P(predict one|one)=.99, P(predict two|two)=.99. Then V_EDT(two)=P(pred two|two)*1000 + P(pred one|two)*1,001,000 ≈ .99*1000 + .01*1,001,000 ≈ 10,990? One ≈ 990,000. So EDT one. Need mention.\n\nAdversarial Offer or psychopath? Need maybe include \"Self-knowledge and adversarial prediction\". Let's pick Psychopath Button because intuitive and source. But mathematical? Could define:\nLet P=psychopath. Prior p low? Paul believes only psychopaths press, so P(P|press) high. Button B. If B=press and not psychopath, outcome no psychopaths (good). If B=press and psychopath, Paul dies (worst). If not press, status quo. CDT evaluates do(press): holds P fixed at prior. If prior p small and benefit large, V_CDT(press) positive. EDT conditions on press and sees high P(P|press), recommends not. Source says CDT endorses pressing and Egan says wrong. Need note. But why Paul quite confident only psychopath would press? If prior p maybe not low? He might not know if psychopath but has evidence? CDT if he has evidence that he is likely psychopath because he feels urge? Joyce says can use information about inclinations as evidence. If before choice he observes strong urge, update prior p. Then CDT may not press. The counterexample assumes no such admissible evidence except the hypothetical act. Need mention. Good.\n\nAdversarial offer: Could mention briefly in criticism: source describes. Maybe not full compute due length. But maybe include as monetary counterexample. Need decide. We can include in criticism rather than examples. Need mention Oesterheld/Conitzer. Maybe enough.\n\nSection: Dependencies and counterfactuals. Need maybe combine with recipe. Could include \"When CDT and EDT agree\". Need maybe after examples.\n\nCriticism section:\n- Vagueness/regress.\n- Counterexamples: Newcomb (source says philosophers disagree), Death in Damascus, adversarial offer, psychopath. Need discuss source claims.\n- My operational view limitations: do-model assumes causal graph known; in predictor problems, the agent's choice may be correlated with predictor through same algorithm; if model includes decision algorithm as common cause, one-shot intervention may still not satisfy \"sane\" because CDT insists not to use action as evidence. There is no universally accepted CDT answer. Possible responses: ratifiability, policy evaluation, update on dispositions. Need mark as proposals.\n\nNeed maybe explain Death in Damascus in more detail:\n\"You are in Damascus, Death appears, says appointment tomorrow in Aleppo? You can flee to Aleppo or stay. Death predicts where you will be and goes there. If reliable, whichever you choose is evidence Death is there. CDT: your current choice does not cause Death's location (already predicted), so no causal benefit; may be indifferent or choose based on tiny travel cost. But intuitive rationality may prefer randomization? Egan says CDT unable to process correlation.\" Need mention.\n\nNeed mention diachronic Dutch books: Oesterheld & Conitzer argue CDT agents can be made to accept sequences of bets losing money. Source. Need maybe not deep.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"statistical independence of A and A→O_j\" from source. Could include in dependencies section: \"If the act is independent of the background variables that make counterfactual true, then the counterfactual probability equals conditional probability. Formally, if P(A □→ O | A)=P(A □→ O) and certain conditional axioms hold, then P(A □→ O)=P(O|A). In randomized trials, this holds.\" Need be cautious. Source says Gibbard/Harper showed if accept two axioms, statistical independence of A and A→O_j suffices to guarantee P(A→O_j)=P(O_j|A). We can quote. Need maybe explain with common cause absent. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"principle of conditional excluded middle\" controversial. Source. Need not dwell.\n\nNeed maybe include \"counterfactual probability of it being the case that A necessarily implies O_j\" maybe weird. Could reinterpret as \"probability that the counterfactual 'if A were taken, O_j would result' is true\". Need note source's wording. Good.\n\nNeed ensure not too many citations. References at end:\n1. Gibbard & Harper 1981.\n2. Lewis 1981.\n3. Lewis 1976.\n4. Egan 2007.\n5. Pearl 2009 (Causality) - additional.\n6. Wikipedia source. Maybe also Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021, Joyce 2012? But references maybe not lengthy. Need maybe include all cited. Target includes references. Could list 8.\n\nNeed check word count after drafting. Let's write in final. But before final, maybe analyze word count. I'll draft in my head with approximate count. Need maybe produce around 2600 words. Use headings and paragraphs. Let's start writing final directly but careful.\n\nPotential final:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Expected Utility Under Intervention\n\nCausal decision theory (CDT) is a family of decision rules... According to the reference article, CDT says rational agent should select action that causes best outcome in expectation; contrasts with EDT... etc.\n\nNeed maybe include \"The reference article leaves open how to compute counterfactual probabilities; much of the operational machinery below is my own.\" Good.\n\nLet's formulate.\n\nDetailed draft:\n\n---\n\nCausal decision theory (CDT) is best understood as the view that choices should be assessed by the expected value of what they would bring about, not by the evidential news they provide. If eating an apple tends to cause happiness and eating an orange causes sadness, CDT recommends the apple. If the apple may be bad, the recommendation depends on expected causal consequences: prior probability it is good, utility of happiness, disutility of sadness. The source article presents this informal idea and the Gibbard-Harper formula. It also notes CDT's contrast with evidential decision theory (EDT), its reliance on counterfactual probabilities, and several objections. The source does not give a complete algorithm for computing the relevant probabilities. In this essay I reconstruct CDT formally, first with counterfactuals and then—my own operational proposal—with causal interventions.\n\nNeed count ~170.\n\nSection 1:\n\"Let A be act variable...\" etc.\n\nNeed include equations.\n\nMaybe use \\square\\to for counterfactual. Need ensure readable.\n\nWrite:\nFor finite outcomes O_j, EDT:\n\\[ V_{EDT}(a)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A=a)D(O_j). \\]\nCDT:\n\\[ V_{CDT}(a)=\\sum_j P(a \\square\\to O_j)D(O_j). \\]\nThe source uses P(A→O_j). D is desirability. The arrow is counterfactual. It asks: how probable is it that if I were to do a, O_j would obtain? Not: how probable is O_j given that I do a? Lewis proved probability of conditional generally not conditional probability. If equal, CDT collapses to EDT.\n\nNeed maybe mention when equal under independence.\n\nSection 2:\nImaging:\nPossible worlds W, prior P. For each w, let w_a be closest A-world. Then P(a □→ O)=∑_w P(w)1_{O}(w_a). If stochastic, kernel. Source says this requires beliefs under certainty of A and may regress. Good.\n\nThen SCM:\nM=(U,V,F,P(U)). For each endogenous variable V_i=F_i(PA_i,U_i). Intervention do(A=a) replaces F_A by constant. Let O_a(u) be value of O in manipulated model with exogenous U=u. Then:\n\\[V_{CDT}(a)=\\sum_u P(u)D(O_a(u))\\]\nor integral. If evidence e observed before choice:\n\\[V_{CDT}(a\\mid e)=\\sum_u P(u\\mid e)D(O_a(u)) = \\sum_o D(o)P(o\\mid do(a),e).\\]\nThis is my proposal. It captures counterfactuals by holding exogenous background fixed while changing action. It distinguishes causes from evidence.\n\nNeed maybe mention model uncertainty:\nIf H hypotheses with prior P(h):\n\\[V_{CDT}(a)=\\sum_h P(h)\\sum_o D(o)P_h(o\\mid do(a)).\\]\nSource's discussion of dependency hypotheses fits this. Egan criticizes holding fixed unconditional credences. We can update P(h|e) with non-action evidence; but CDT does not replace P(h) by P(h|a). Need mark.\n\nSection 3 recipe:\n- Step 1: specify decision problem.\n- Step 2: causal graph.\n- Step 3: compute interventional distribution. Use adjustment: if Z backdoor satisfies, P(o|do(a))=∑_zP(o|a,z)P(z). If no confounding, equals conditional. If front-door, etc. If impossible, use simulation/structural equations.\n- Step 4: maximize. For policies: π maps histories to actions; evaluate V(π)=E_{do(π)}[D(O)]. In sequential setting, do(π) sets each action variable to π_t of prior variables. Observations remain generated by model. This treats feedback properly.\n\nNeed maybe include \"relevant dependencies\" in this section: CDT keeps causal dependencies downstream of action; severs incoming arrows to action. Common causes remain. Colliders etc. Need maybe write.\n\nSection examples:\nApple: p good. U_apple=pD(H)+(1-p)D(S). If orange known sad U_orange=D(S_o). No confounding, EDT=CDT.\n\nDavid: C charisma, A summon, R revolt. C affects R and disposition to A; A does not affect C; unjust acts don't cause R. Let q=P(C=ch), r_c, r_u revolt probabilities, b benefit of Bathsheba, c cost revolt. CDT's causal probability of revolt under do(summon) is q r_c+(1-q)r_u (assuming summoning itself doesn't change revolt risk). EDT uses P(C|A=summon), overweighting uncharismatic. Thus EDT may abstain; CDT may summon if b>c*baseline risk. Source says CDT recommends sending; this illustrates treating charisma as fixed background. Need maybe mention if David has evidence about charisma, both update prior.\n\nNewcomb: predictor, boxes. Let q prior probability predictor predicted one-box. Contents already set. Under do(one): V= q*1M+(1-q)*0. Under do(two): q*1,001,000+(1-q)*1,000. Difference = 1,000, so two-box. This is not because CDT thinks two-boxing is evidence of empty box; it ignores that evidence. EDT with 99% accuracy yields one-box: V_EDT(one)≈0.99*1M, V_EDT(two)≈0.99*1000+0.01*1,001,000 ≈ 10,990. Source's claim. Need note philosophers disagree.\n\nPsychopath button: variable P. Press B. If B causes elimination of psychopaths; if Paul is psychopath, pressing causes his death. Paul believes only psychopath would press, so P(P|B=press) high. CDT: do(B=press) holds P fixed at prior/admissible posterior. If prior P(P) low and world without psychopaths valuable, V_CDT(press) may exceed not pressing. EDT conditions on B and recommends not. Source/Egan says CDT endorses pressing, widely counterintuitive. Need mention Joyce response: if Paul has actual evidence of psychopathic inclinations, CDT should condition on that evidence before intervention. But counterexample relies on act itself being evidence.\n\nMaybe include Adversarial Offer in criticism: Source says CDT buys box because causal expected value >=1.50. Need explain as adversarial prediction. Could include short paragraph: \"Similarly, Adversarial Offer...\" Need maybe word count.\n\nSection Dependencies and agreement:\nNeed maybe earlier? Could have a subsection after examples: \"What counts as a relevant dependency?\" Explain CDT doesn't ignore all correlations; it ignores correlations that are not causal pathways from action to outcome, but it uses correlations that are consequences of action or mediated by action. If action causes a symptom that predicts outcome, symptom is downstream; intervention includes it. If common cause, no. If action changes a variable that then changes outcome, include. If policy uses observation, observation is not intervened upon; it's part of causal process. Good.\n\nCould include in recipe.\n\nCriticism/open questions:\n- Vagueness/imaging regress source.\n- Counterexamples. Death in Damascus: need explain. CDT may not know to flee? If Death predicts, action doesn't cause Death's location. If staying has no travel cost and fleeing maybe cost, CDT might stay, but if Death reliable and predicted flee, staying leads death? Wait scenario: Death says \"I have appointment with you tomorrow in Damascus\" maybe? Let's recall classic: You are in Damascus, Death appears, you flee to Aleppo, Death says \"I have appointment with you tomorrow in Aleppo\"? Actually story: Death in Damascus: A man sees Death in Damascus, flees to Aleppo to avoid Death, but Death predicted he would flee to Aleppo and is waiting there; if he had stayed, Death would have been in Damascus? The rational action unclear. CDT: if Death's location already fixed by prediction, choosing location doesn't causally affect survival; may randomize? Source says CDT agent unable to process correlation, may make irrational decisions. Need state source claim not detailed. Could say: In Death in Damascus, a reliable predictor places Death where you will be. Your choice is correlated with Death's location but does not cause it. Standard one-shot CDT sees no causal difference between options, or only small costs, while intuitively the correlation seems decision-relevant. This is one of Egan's criticisms. Good.\n- Adversarial Offer: CDT buys box but seller profits. Need maybe explain: two boxes, cost $1, seller puts $3 in boxes predicted not bought, accuracy .75. CDT reasons chosen box's expected contents > cost because under intervention prediction fixed? Actually source: at least one box contains $3, average box at least $1.50, so buy. But because buyer can buy only one, seller's prediction makes chosen box likely empty? Need maybe not over-explain.\n- Oesterheld/Conitzer diachronic Dutch books.\n- My operational interpretation doesn't solve normative disputes; it clarifies computation given a causal model. If the model says predictor is prior common cause, CDT two-boxes; if one thinks rationality requires exploiting predictable correlations, one may prefer EDT or alternative (FDT etc not in source). Need maybe not mention FDT unless own? Could mention \"other theories\" but not needed.\n\nConclusion: CDT's core: maximize expected utility under intervention; clear distinction from EDT; operationalized by do; unresolved counterfactual computation and counterexamples.\n\nReferences: Need list. Could include source and primary.\n\nNeed ensure no unsupported claim \"Pearl\" but own. Need maybe cite Pearl 2009 as additional. Need references not too long. Maybe:\n- Ahmed 2021 (source)\n- Gibbard & Harper 1981\n- Lewis 1976\n- Lewis 1981\n- Egan 2007\n- Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021\n- Pearl 2009 (my addition)\n- Wikipedia revision (source). But if include Wikipedia, okay.\n\nNeed maybe not include Joyce because not necessary? Could include Joyce 2012 if mention. Source includes. Need references count 8. Word count maybe okay.\n\nNeed be careful with \"P(A -> O_j) is the counterfactual probability of it being the case that A necessarily implies O_j.\" Might need explain: \"necessarily\" in closest possible worlds, not logical. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"principle of conditional excluded middle\" mention? Could mention briefly in independence section. Not necessary but source says. We can say \"Gibbard and Harper showed, under two axioms (one connected to conditional excluded middle), statistical independence ...\" Good.\n\nNeed perhaps mention \"if actions and conditionals are not independent\" as David case. Good.\n\nLet's now consider possible word count if final around 2600. Need maybe write more compact but detailed. Let's estimate final draft length if I write all above. Might be ~3000. Need avoid too long. But target up to 3000, okay. Need maybe 2700. Let's produce polished.\n\nPotential issue: formula V_CDT(two) in Newcomb if predictor accuracy and prior q? If q is prior probability predicted one. If predictor is accurate and agent likely one-box? But CDT uses prior q. Difference always 1000 if same q. Good. If predictor predicts with 99% conditional on actual choice, the correlation is not causal; but q may be determined by agent's disposition. In one-shot choice, q is unconditional credence. Good.\n\nPotential issue: David example: If summoning Bathsheba is unjust act and uncharismatic kings tend to act unjustly, does CDT treat action as not caused by charisma? In decision theory, act is under agent's control; but if charisma influences propensity, then intervention do(A) can still be applied. If charisma causes A, cutting incoming arrows makes A independent. But if agent's prior over charisma should be updated by the fact that they are choosing A? CDT says no. Good.\n\nPotential issue: SCM with action variable having causes (e.g. psychological traits) and intervention do(A) severs them. Some philosophers object this is too coarse. Need mention in criticisms: intervention on action variable assumes actions are exogenous at moment of choice; in cases where choice is strongly predicted by traits, whether to hold traits fixed is exactly disputed. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"policy\" evaluation in Death in Damascus: If predictor predicts policy, evaluating policy might produce randomization? CDT may randomize if mixed policy has causal expected value? Need maybe mention as possible operational response. For adversarial predictor, if action is chosen by stochastic policy, predictor predicts distribution? CDT can evaluate mixed strategies by intervention on random seed? If predictor can predict random seed? Hmm. Could mention: CDT can handle randomization by including random seed as exogenous; if predictor knows seed, correlation remains. But not necessary.\n\nNeed maybe include \"counterfactuals after evidence\" example: If you learn apple is good, posterior changes. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"decision problem with policies\" explicitly: alternative policies, not just acts. Formula:\nFor policy π in dynamic model with action variables A_t and observations X_t, V(π)=E[D(O) | do(A_t=π_t(X_{<t},A_{<t}) for all t))]. This is precise. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"causal sufficiency\"? Not necessary.\n\nNeed maybe include \"CDT and EDT agree in randomized experiments\" as example. Good.\n\nNeed ensure final not too many equations? Mathematically literate audience, okay.\n\nLet's now write final with headings. Need maintain essay tone. Need cite source claims. Need mark own proposals: \"I will assume\", \"not in source\".\n\nPotential final text:\n\n---\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Expected Utility under Intervention\n\nCausal decision theory (CDT) ... The reference article... I will separate source claims from my reconstruction.\n\n## 1. Two expected utilities\n...\n\n## 2. Counterfactual probabilities: imaging and intervention\n...\n\n## 3. An operational procedure\n...\n\n## 4. Examples\n...\n\n## 5. Dependencies, policies, and when CDT agrees with EDT\n...\n\n## 6. Open problems and counterexamples\n...\n\n## Conclusion\n\nReferences.\n\nNeed maybe combine sections to save words. But okay.\n\nLet's draft final carefully.\n\nNeed maybe include source oldid reference in references.\n\nLet's ensure no citation needed for Pearl? We can say \"A common modern way, not mentioned in the source but widely used, is... (Pearl 2009).\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Lewis's triviality result\" with source footnote. Could say \"Lewis (1976) proved that probabilities of conditionals cannot generally be identified with conditional probabilities; indeed under closure under conditionalization only trivial probability functions allow such identification.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"conditional excluded middle\" in independence. Could say \"Gibbard and Harper showed that under two axioms, one related to controversial principle of conditional excluded middle, if A is statistically independent of the counterfactual A→O_j, then the counterfactual probability equals the conditional probability.\" Good. Need maybe not overexplain.\n\nPotential word count: Let's write final then estimate. Need maybe use concise references. Let's start.\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer user's request: write a mathematical essay/blog post explaining CDT and how to apply it to decision problems, using supplied Wikipedia source. Need target 2400-3000 words including references. Need careful. Need likely produce final essay only. Need think in English (user English). Need include definitions, equations, examples, uncertainty, source vs own assumptions. Need distinguish source claims vs own proposals. Need maybe operational interpretation using causal Bayesian networks / do-calculus? The source mentions counterfactual probabilities but no algorithm. We can develop reasonable operational interpretation: use causal model, interventions, expected utility under do(A), plus dependency hypotheses if structural uncertainty. Need mention Lewis imaging, counterfactuals, possible worlds, and causal Bayes net do-operator as practical method, noting not in source but own proposal. Need be mathematically precise enough reader could decide unfamiliar cases. Need discuss evaluating alternative actions/policies, dependencies, counterfactuals. Need mention EDT formula and difference. Examples: apple/orange, David/Bathsheba, Newcomb, Death in Damascus maybe, Adversarial Offer, psychopath button? Need keep word count ~2400-3000. Need include references brief maybe source and some canonical works. Avoid lengthy bibliography. Need maybe 2600 words. Need no appendix. Need final in essay style with headings. Need distinguish source claims. Could have sections: 1 What CDT says; 2 Formal decision rule; 3 Counterfactual probabilities and interventions; 4 CDT vs EDT; 5 Examples; 6 Policies and dependency hypotheses; 7 Open questions and operational proposal; 8 Criticisms; conclusion; references.\n\nNeed be careful about formula: Source: U(A)=sum_j P(A -> O_j) D(O_j). P(A -> O_j) counterfactual probability of it being case that A necessarily implies O_j. That's awkward; could express as probability of counterfactual \"if A were done, O_j would occur\". Need mention Lewis: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability generally. Could formalize with imaging: P(A □→ O) = sum_w P(w) P_A(w?) Actually Lewis imaging: P_A(O) = sum_w P(w) I_A(O) where shift mass to closest A-worlds. Or define P(A □→ O) = P(K_A(O)) with imaging kernel. But then propose operational: causal Bayesian network: U(A) = E[u | do(A)] = sum_o u(o) P(o | do(A)). For actions with uncertainty over causal graphs: U(A)=sum_h P(h) E[u | do(A), h] perhaps holding fixed prior over dependency hypotheses? Egan criticism says CDT holds fixed unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses; but maybe in dynamic choice should update on intention? Need discuss. Need distinguish source vs own. Need maybe use Pearl do calculus. But source doesn't mention Pearl. We can say additional assumption: when available, a causal DAG gives an operational semantics. Need explain backdoor adjustment if confounders: P(o | do(a)) = sum_z P(o | a,z) P(z) if Z satisfies backdoor. This helps decide unfamiliar cases. Need discuss policies: if evaluating policy π mapping info to actions, use do(π) or sequential interventions; expected utility of policy under causal model. Need mention dependencies: common causes, confounders, mediators, colliders. CDT cuts incoming arrows to action (intervention) but retains causal effects downstream, doesn't condition on evidence caused by action except as part of outcome. Need mention counterfactuals: for actual observed world, can evaluate action by counterfactual outcome given evidence, but for ex ante choice use prior expected intervention. Need talk about Newcomb: CDT two-box because do(two) doesn't change predictor's prior prediction; if predictor's prediction caused by common cause? Need compute: U(one)=P(predict one)*1M + P(predict two)*0? Under do(one), distribution of prediction remains prior P(predict one). If predictor highly accurate and prior choice? Need if no action affects prediction, expected utility two = P(predict one)*1,001,000 + P(predict two)*1,000? Actually if P(predict one) maybe depends on agent's disposition? Ex ante, agent's credence about predictor's prediction. CDT holds fixed. If predictor accurate with 99%, but choice not causal, two-box dominates: in each prediction state, two yields $1000 more. So CDT two-box. EDT one-box because conditional P(predict one|one) high. Need mention. Death in Damascus: CDT may choose to stay/flee? Source says CDT unable to process correlation; need maybe explain: if Death predicts your choice and goes there, fleeing is evidence but not cause; CDT sees no causal benefit? If Death's location is set by prediction, then action cannot affect Death's location, but if you stay and Death in Damascus you die; if flee and Death in Aleppo? Need if you don't know where Death will be? Need maybe not dwell. Adversarial Offer: Need explain source's example: two boxes each cost $1, seller put $3 in each box predicted not acquire, accuracy .75. Buyer can purchase one or none not both. CDT reasons at least one box contains $3? Actually if seller predicts buyer not to acquire each box independently? If buyer chooses a box, causal expected value of chosen box? Because seller's prediction about that box is correlated with buyer's choice through common cause? CDT holds fixed prediction? Need parse: seller predicted buyer not to acquire each box. If buyer considers buying box 1, under do(buy 1), probability box1 contains $3 is P(seller predicted not buy 1). If accuracy 0.75? But because can't buy both, maybe at least one predicted not acquire? Source says CDT reasons at least one box contains $3. Therefore average box at least $1.50 in causal expected value, > cost, buy one. But seller profits. Need maybe mention as alleged counterexample where CDT's causal calculation ignores selection/adversarial correlation. Need be careful not too long.\n\nNeed include mathematical definitions:\n- Decision problem: finite actions A, outcomes O, utility u:O→R, states/word variables.\n- EDT: V_EDT(a)=Σ_o u(o) P(o|a) (if P(a)>0).\n- CDT Gibbard-Harper: V_CDT(a)=Σ_o u(o) P(a □→ o) where □→ counterfactual. Or P(A -> O). Need mention probabilities of counterfactuals.\n- Imaging: Given possible worlds W, prior P, closeness ordering, define P_a(o)=Σ_{w∈W} P(w) 1[o holds at f_a(w)] where f_a(w) is closest a-world to w. Then V_CDT(a)=Σ_o u(o)P_a(o).\n- Causal model: structural causal model (U,V,F) or DAG. Intervention do(A=a) sets A to a, replaces structural equation. Then P(o | do(a)) = distribution. V_CDT(a)=Σ_o u(o) P(o | do(a)). This is my operational proposal. Need justify: intervention captures counterfactual probability; do is precise; can compute via graph. Need mention when causal model uncertain: average over models/hypotheses H: V(a)=Σ_h P(h) Σ_o u(o) P_h(o | do(a)). But Egan says holding fixed initial dependency hypotheses can be problematic; discuss maybe dynamic deliberation. Need maybe propose ratifiable CDT? Hmm. Source says Egan argues disconnect and holding fixed unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses leads irrational. Need mention uncertainty. Could propose operational: use current best causal credences, but if action provides evidence about hypotheses because of self-prediction, CDT still doesn't condition on action; however if choosing policy, evaluate policy by its causal consequences under hypotheses; if you expect to learn, incorporate that as observation nodes. Need maybe discuss policy evaluation: policy π maps observations to actions; evaluate by intervening to set policy (or implementing controller) and expected utility. For sequential decisions, use influence diagrams / dynamic programming. Need mention if action is not atomic but policy, do(π) means forcing the policy's actions at each decision point; keep observations generated naturally. This treats dependencies appropriately: action can influence future observations via causal paths, but not exogenous variables.\n\nNeed discuss relevant dependencies and counterfactuals: CDT distinguishes causal from evidential dependencies. It respects common cause correlations but does not exploit them as reasons to act unless action changes cause? Example David: charisma common cause of just acts and revolt. Summoning Bathsheba is unjust act but doesn't cause revolt; charisma fixed. CDT says send for her if desire outcome? Need compute: EDT abstain because abstaining evidence charismatic/no revolt? Actually source: EDT recommends abstain, CDT recommends sending. Need explain. David wants Bathsheba, fears revolt. Charismatic kings act justly and low revolt; uncharismatic act unjustly and high revolt. Unjust acts don't cause revolt; charisma causes both. David doesn't know charisma. If summoning is unjust, choosing summon is evidence uncharismatic and revolt; EDT abstains. But CDT: do(summon) does not change charisma, and unjust act not cause revolt; if he wants Bathsheba enough, expected causal utility of summoning high; charisma fixed. Need mention.\n\nNeed also discuss when CDT = EDT: if action probabilistically independent of dependency hypotheses / states relevant to outcome under intervention, or Gibbard-Harper axioms statistical independence of A and A→O_j yields equality. Source says if accept two axioms then statistical independence suffices. Need explain: when no common causes connecting action and outcomes except through action, conditional probability equals counterfactual probability. In randomized experiments, do(a) distribution equals P(o|a). In confounded cases, differ.\n\nNeed discuss Lewis's result: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability, triviality. We can mention but not deep.\n\nNeed explain \"if A necessarily implies O_j\" from source maybe ambiguous; use counterfactual \"if A were performed, O_j would obtain\". Need maybe note source's phrase \"necessarily implies\" should not be read as logical necessity but as counterfactual dependence in closest worlds/intervention.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical example with table. Apple: states good/bad, prior p. Actions eat apple/eat orange? Define utilities. CDT: U(apple)=p u(happy)+(1-p)u(sad) if eating apple's causal effects conditional on state. If orange known causes sad? Need maybe simple.\n\nNeed maybe present an algorithmic recipe:\n1. Specify actions, outcomes, utility.\n2. Specify causal model or dependency hypotheses (DAG/SCM), including variables for states, predictions, traits.\n3. For each action, compute interventional distribution P(o | do(a)), not P(o | a), by cutting arrows into action, using adjustment or simulation.\n4. Compute expected utility and choose max.\n5. For policies, evaluate do(policy) and choose max. If model uncertainty, average over models with prior (or updated but not action-conditioned? Need discuss).\nNeed mention how to handle observations: if action is chosen after evidence e, use conditional interventional distribution P(o | do(a), e) or posterior over exogenous variables given e. That's important for applying to unfamiliar cases. Need be precise: after observing evidence E=e not caused by action, update beliefs about exogenous variables U; then evaluate do(a) in posterior SCM. If evidence is caused by action or downstream, careful not to double count? In decision at time t, condition on information available up to t, then intervene on future action. Formula: V(a | e)=Σ_o u(o) P(o | do(a), e) where e is pre-action evidence. In SCM, update posterior over exogenous noise given e, then set A=a. This is Pearl's effect of causes on effects given evidence. Need include.\n\nNeed discuss policies with feedback: If policy uses observations, evaluating policy π uses intervention on action variables as functions of observations; not simply one-shot do(A=a). Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe distinguish claims: Source claims CDT recommends action causing best expected outcome, formula, difference from EDT, Lewis, David, Newcomb, criticisms, vagueness. My additions: SCM/do-calculus operationalization, adjustment formula, policy evaluation, decision recipe, some normative comments. Need explicitly flag.\n\nNeed consider target word count. Need maybe 2500 words. Let's plan final essay.\n\nPotential title: \"Causal Decision Theory: Expected Utility Under Intervention\".\n\nStructure:\n1. Introduction (200 words)\n2. Expected utility and two probability kernels (350 words)\n   - EDT formula, CDT formula, explanation.\n3. Counterfactuals and interventions (450 words)\n   - imaging and do-operator. Proposed operational interpretation.\n4. How to apply CDT: a recipe (450 words)\n   - variables, causal graph, intervention, adjustment, uncertainty, policies.\n5. Examples (600 words)\n   - Apple/orange (simple), David/Bathsheba (common cause), Newcomb (prediction), maybe Adversarial Offer or psychopath button briefly.\n6. Dependencies, counterfactuals, and when CDT=EDT (250 words)\n7. Open problems/criticisms (300 words)\n   - vagueness, Egan, Death in Damascus, Dutch books, ratifiability. My operational interpretation limitations.\n8. Conclusion (100)\nReferences (150?) Total maybe 2900. Need keep not too long. Could be 2600. Need include references to source and primary works. Need not lengthy bibliography. Maybe references list with 6-8 items.\n\nNeed maybe mention mathematical notation:\nLet A be action variable with values a. Let O outcome variable, U utility. EDT:\nV_EDT(a)=E[U | A=a] = Σ_o u(o)P(O=o | A=a).\nCDT:\nV_CDT(a)=Σ_o u(o)P(O=o □→ A=a) or P(a □→ o). Use box arrow.\nIn SCM M=(U,V,F,P(U)): do(A=a) yields M_a. P_a(o)=P_{M_a}(O=o). Then V_CDT(a)=E_{M_a}[u(O)]. If observed evidence e prior to action: update P(u|e), then V(a|e)=Σ_u P(u|e) u(O_{M_a}(u)). Or equivalently P(o | do(a), e).\nAdjustment: If Z backdoor, P(o | do(a)) = Σ_z P(o | a,z)P(z). If front-door etc. If no confounding, P(o | do(a))=P(o|a).\nPolicy: π: X→A. Evaluate V(π)=E_{M_{do(π)}}[u(O)], where do(π) sets A_t=π_t(H_t). If deterministic, can be represented as intervention on action nodes.\n\nNeed maybe discuss dependency hypotheses H. Source/Egan: CDT uses unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses. Formal: V_CDT(a)=Σ_h P(h) Σ_o u(o)P_h(o|do(a),h). But if action is evidence about h (e.g. predictor predicts action), EDT would condition P(h|a); CDT does not. But after observing evidence not caused by action, update P(h|e). Need mention.\n\nNeed perhaps clarify \"causes best outcome in expectation\" doesn't mean actual guaranteed best. It uses expected utility relative to agent's causal beliefs. It can recommend action that turns out badly (bad apple). Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"news\" EDT: choose action that would be good news. CDT chooses action that would be good intervention.\n\nNeed mention Gibbard & Harper axioms and independence: if A independent of counterfactual A→O? Hmm source: statistical independence of A and A→O_j suffices under axioms to guarantee P(A→O_j)=P(O_j|A). Actually maybe if actions independent of states? We can state in less technical way: if action is not correlated with the background conditions that determine the counterfactual, conditional probabilities coincide. Need avoid inaccurate. Could phrase: Under certain conditional logic assumptions, if the act A is probabilistically independent of the counterfactual connection between A and O, EDT and CDT agree. In many ordinary decisions this holds.\n\nNeed be careful with P(A -> O_j): source says probability of counterfactual that A necessarily implies O_j. Lewis proved probability of conditional not equal conditional probability. We can say the arrow is a counterfactual conditional, not material conditional.\n\nNeed maybe discuss possible-world imaging: Let W set of worlds. For each world w and action a, let w_a be selected closest a-world. Then P_a(O)=Σ_w P(w)1_O(w_a). If stochastic closeness, kernel K_a(w' | w). Then P_a(O)=Σ_w P(w)K_a({w':O}|w). This addresses regress? Source says imaging requires knowing what believe if certain of A; can lead regress. Need mention as source claim. Our do-operator avoids by assuming causal model; but still requires model selection. Need distinguish.\n\nNeed maybe discuss policies and Dutch books? Could say CDT can be Dutch-booked in certain predictor cases if it treats predictions as fixed but then updates after choice? Oesterheld & Conitzer argue diachronic Dutch books. Need mention source. But not elaborate too much.\n\nNeed maybe include \"ratifiability\"? Not in source except Harper title. Could mention as possible response: choose action that remains optimal conditional on intending it. But not necessary. If mention, mark as beyond source. Need maybe avoid too much.\n\nNeed write essay not answer one case. Need likely no direct \"I\" maybe okay. Need include references at end.\n\nNeed ensure no hallucinated source claims? Need mark own. For source, reference Wikipedia. Need maybe state \"The reference article emphasizes...\". Need include citations in text like (Gibbard & Harper 1981), (Lewis 1981), (Egan 2007). Need references list.\n\nNeed produce around 2600 words. Let's draft mentally. Need count? We'll estimate. Could use headings. Need final answer only essay. Need maybe include equations. Need no markdown table? Could use bullet list? Essay. Need include references. Need maybe target 2400-3000. Let's aim ~2600. Need count maybe:\nTitle + intro 200\nSection 1 350\nSection 2 450\nSection 3 500\nSection 4 examples 700\nSection 5 criticism 350\nConclusion 100\nReferences 150 = 2800.\n\nLet's draft with rich detail but not overly repetitive.\n\nPotential essay:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory as Expected Utility under Intervention\n\nIntro: decision theory, CDT, EDT. Source claim. CDT says choose act with best expected causal consequences. It is not about actual known consequences; expected. Need mathematical literate.\n\nSection: Two expectations. Define finite outcomes. EDT formula. CDT formula with counterfactual probability. Discuss D(O_j). The arrow. If probabilities conditional, CDT collapses to EDT; Lewis triviality says not generally. Gibbard-Harper.\n\nSection: What are counterfactual probabilities? Imaging and interventions.\n- Lewis imaging formula. Source says vague/regress.\n- My proposal: SCM/do. Define M, variables, structural equations, exogenous distribution. Intervention do(A=a). Formula V_CDT. Relationship to imaging: selecting closest worlds corresponds to holding fixed exogenous variables except act? Need maybe mention. In deterministic SCM, counterfactual outcome O_a(u). Expected utility = Σ_u P(u) u(O_a(u)). This is twin network? Good.\n- If model uncertain: H. Formula. Need mention source doesn't specify algorithm; this is my addition.\n- Evidence: V(a|e)=Σ_u P(u|e)u(O_a(u)). Need explain not P(o|a,e) generally.\n\nSection: Applying CDT: recipe.\n1 identify actions/outcomes/utility.\n2 build causal graph; mark exogenous/predictors/common causes. Action node has no causes except maybe decision? In SCM, intervention cuts incoming arrows.\n3 compute interventional distribution. Backdoor adjustment. If randomized, equals conditional. If confounded, adjust.\n4 maximize. For policies, evaluate policy intervention. Need include formula for sequential policy. Treat observations as variables affected by previous actions, not as evidence to condition the action's causal efficacy? Actually evaluate with do policy. Need mention.\n\nSection: Examples.\nApple: p good. If eat apple: U=pH+(1-p)S. Orange maybe known sad. Need simple.\nDavid: variables C charisma, A summon, R revolt, B pleasure. C causes A? Actually C influences disposition, but action A not caused? David's action may be influenced by C? In decision, action is free; but C correlates with choice? EDT treats A evidence of C. Graph C→A? If C causes tendencies, but decision can override? For decision, we treat A as intervention, C fixed. Graph C→R, C→A_tendency maybe. A not cause R. Compute EDT vs CDT. Need explain.\nNewcomb: variable P prediction, A choice, B contents. Predictor sets B based on P. Graph P? Actually predictor predicts A before; if perfect predictor, common cause? In causal graph, A? Temporal: predictor's prediction causes box content, prediction is caused by predictor's model of agent maybe same factors as choice? CDT says do(A=two) doesn't change prediction. Dominance: if predicted one, two gives 1,001,000 vs one 1,000,000; if predicted two, two gives 1,000 vs one 0. So two dominates. EDT: conditional probabilities. Need mention if predictor accuracy 99%, V_EDT(one)≈0.99*1M, V_EDT(two)≈0.99*1000? Need depending. Source says 99% accuracy. Need maybe compute if prior? If predictor accurate, P(B=1M | one)=0.99. V_CDT(two)=P(pred one)*1,001,000 + P(pred two)*1,000; V_CDT(one)=P(pred one)*1,000,000+P(pred two)*0; difference 1000 >0. Good.\nPsychopath button or adversarial offer? Need maybe include one more to illustrate counterexample. Psychopath: variable P psychopath, A press, outcome death/no psychopaths. Press causes no psychopaths if not psychopath? Actually if Paul psychopath, pressing? It kills all psychopaths including Paul? He prefers living with psychopaths to dying. He believes only psychopath would press. CDT: if press, causes psychopaths killed; holding psychopathy fixed? If Paul is psychopath, pressing causes his death, terrible; if not, good. But he is confident only psychopath would press, so P(psychopath|press) high. EDT says don't. CDT? Source says CDT endorses pressing. Why? If do(press), does it change probability psychopath? No, holds fixed prior? But if prior Paul quite confident only psychopath would press? Hmm prior that he is psychopath maybe? He is quite confident only psychopath would press, not that he is psychopath. If he considers pressing, under do(press) his psychopathy remains prior low? Then outcome good. CDT says press. But if his choice is evidence of psychopathy via common cause, CDT ignores. Need mention as counterexample. Maybe better use Death in Damascus or Adversarial Offer. Source gives Adversarial Offer with numbers, can compute. Need maybe include both? Word constraints. Maybe mention psychopath briefly as example of common-cause self-knowledge. Need not compute extensively. But user asks use examples whenever helpful. We can include three: apple, David/Newcomb, Adversarial or psychopath. Need maybe enough.\n\nSection: Criticism and open questions.\n- vagueness: no algorithm; imaging regress. Source. Our do-calculus addresses but requires caus