be Wikipedia source as reference. But target includes references. Could list 8.\n\nNeed ensure target words. Let's write. Need maybe use LaTeX inline. Need be readable.\n\nPotential details for mathematical operationalization:\n\nSCM: M=(\\mathbf{U},\\mathbf{V},F,P(\\mathbf{U})). Each V_i=F_i(PA_i,U_i). Action A variable. Intervention do(A=a) creates M_a with F_A replaced by constant a. Counterfactual outcome O_a(u)=O_{M_a}(u). Then V_CDT(a)=∑_u P(u)D(O_a(u)). If O discrete. If continuous integral. If observed evidence e: posterior P(u|e) and V(a|e)=∑_u P(u|e)D(O_a(u)). This is precise.\nNeed mention if action is impossible in some worlds? do handles if allowed; if impossible maybe undefined. Source imaging assumes A possible.\n\nDependency hypotheses H: each h is an SCM or causal graph. V(a)=∑_h P(h) V_h(a). Egan's criticism: using prior P(h) can be bad if action provides evidence about h. But CDT says action is not evidence about causal structure for purposes of causal expected utility. We can propose: use posterior P(h|e) from admissible evidence e, but not P(h|a). If the agent's act is predicted by h, there is tension. Need distinguish.\n\nBackdoor formula: If graph has confounder Z affecting A and O, then P(o|do(a))=∑_z P(o|a,z)P(z). Need maybe example: David charisma Z? If A=summon, O=revolt, Z=charisma. Since charisma affects A tendency and revolt, but action not cause revolt? To compute causal effect of summoning on revolt, if charisma is confounder and we have data, adjust: P(revolt|do(summon))=∑_c P(revolt|summon,c)P(c). But if summoning itself doesn't cause revolt, conditional P(revolt|summon,c) maybe independent of summoning? Then result is baseline revolt probability for charisma distribution. EDT uses P(revolt|summon) which overweights uncharismatic. Need nice.\n\nNeed maybe include dominance principle: If one action causally dominates (better in every state under intervention), CDT chooses it. Newcomb two-box dominates because contents fixed relative to intervention. Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe clarify actions vs policies: If evaluating policy in Newcomb-like predictor that predicts policy, one-shot CDT may still two-box if prediction fixed before implementation. If predictor can predict policy and you choose policy before predictor? Hmm. Need not.\n\nPotential risk: user asks \"Explain how the theory evaluates alternative actions or policies and how it treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Where the source leaves an important question open, explain uncertainty and, if you think a reasonable operational interpretation is available, develop and justify it. Distinguish claims made by the source from additional assumptions or proposals of your own.\" Need explicit distinction. I can have paragraphs: \"The source claims...\"; \"The source does not specify... I will use...\". Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"target approximately 2,400–3,000 words\". Need ensure final length. Let's approximate as I write. Use concise but detailed.\n\nLet's begin drafting final.\n\nTitle: Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by the Expected Consequences of Interventions\n\nIntro maybe 170 words.\n\nSection 1: The basic rule.\n\nMathematical definitions. Need maybe use D for desirability. Need mention expected utility.\n\nPotential text:\n\"Let \\mathcal{A} be finite set of acts, \\mathcal{O} outcomes, D:\\mathcal{O}\\to\\mathbb{R}. EDT uses conditional probability: V_EDT(A)=∑_j P(O_j|A)D(O_j). CDT, following Gibbard and Harper, uses V_CDT(A)=∑_j P(A \\square\\to O_j)D(O_j).\" Need use source arrow maybe A \\to O_j but explain counterfactual. Need maybe note \"the arrow must not be read as material implication.\" Good.\n\nNeed discuss Lewis: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability. If they were equal, CDT=EDT. Source.\n\nSection 2: Interpretations of counterfactual probabilities. Imaging. SCM. Need write:\n\"The reference notes CDT does not specify algorithm. Lewis proposed imaging: P(A □→ O)=∑_w P(w)1_O(w_A) ... This can be stochastic. But Shaffer's regress... Source. I propose using causal models.\" Need maybe cite Pearl as additional.\n\nSCM formula. Need write in detail.\n\nSection 3: How to decide. Recipe with subheadings.\nNeed include:\n- ex ante vs after evidence.\n- model uncertainty.\n- policies.\n\nSection 4: Examples.\nUse equations.\n\nApple example:\n\"Suppose prior p apple good. If eat apple causes happiness H if good, sadness S if bad; orange causes sadness S_o. V(apple)=pD(H)+(1-p)D(S). V(orange)=D(S_o). If V(apple)>V(orange), eat apple. This is just expected utility, because no confounding. If apple's quality is a common cause? Not relevant.\" Good.\n\nDavid example with causal graph. Could include equation:\nLet C∈{ch, un}. P(C=ch)=q. Revolt probabilities r_ch, r_un with r_ch<r_un. Utility of summoning includes pleasure b and revolt cost c. CDT:\nV_CDT(summon)=q[D(success? maybe no revolt)??]. Need simpler: Because A does not causally affect C and A does not cause revolt except perhaps through? Then P(revolt | do(summon)) = q r_ch + (1-q) r_un (if no causal effect of unjust act). EDT: P(revolt|summon)=P(C=un|summon)r_un + P(C=ch|summon)r_ch, and since uncharismatic more likely to summon, P(C=un|summon)>P(C=un), making revolt more likely. CDT may summon if benefit outweighs baseline risk; EDT abstain if summoning is too strong evidence of uncharisma. Good. Need note source says CDT recommends sending for her. We can assume utility.\n\nNewcomb:\nLet q=P(predict one). Under do(one): contents fixed. V_CDT(one)=q*1,000,000+(1-q)*0. V_CDT(two)=q*1,001,000+(1-q)*1,000 = V_CDT(one)+1,000. Therefore two. EDT: V_EDT(one)≈P(predict one|one)*1M ≈ .99M; V_EDT(two)≈P(predict one|two)*1M? Actually if two, box B empty plus 1000, so ≈ .01*1M? Need if accuracy 99: P(predict one|one)=.99, P(predict two|two)=.99. Then V_EDT(two)=P(pred two|two)*1000 + P(pred one|two)*1,001,000 ≈ .99*1000 + .01*1,001,000 ≈ 10,990? One ≈ 990,000. So EDT one. Need mention.\n\nAdversarial Offer or psychopath? Need maybe include \"Self-knowledge and adversarial prediction\". Let's pick Psychopath Button because intuitive and source. But mathematical? Could define:\nLet P=psychopath. Prior p low? Paul believes only psychopaths press, so P(P|press) high. Button B. If B=press and not psychopath, outcome no psychopaths (good). If B=press and psychopath, Paul dies (worst). If not press, status quo. CDT evaluates do(press): holds P fixed at prior. If prior p small and benefit large, V_CDT(press) positive. EDT conditions on press and sees high P(P|press), recommends not. Source says CDT endorses pressing and Egan says wrong. Need note. But why Paul quite confident only psychopath would press? If prior p maybe not low? He might not know if psychopath but has evidence? CDT if he has evidence that he is likely psychopath because he feels urge? Joyce says can use information about inclinations as evidence. If before choice he observes strong urge, update prior p. Then CDT may not press. The counterexample assumes no such admissible evidence except the hypothetical act. Need mention. Good.\n\nAdversarial offer: Could mention briefly in criticism: source describes. Maybe not full compute due length. But maybe include as monetary counterexample. Need decide. We can include in criticism rather than examples. Need mention Oesterheld/Conitzer. Maybe enough.\n\nSection: Dependencies and counterfactuals. Need maybe combine with recipe. Could include \"When CDT and EDT agree\". Need maybe after examples.\n\nCriticism section:\n- Vagueness/regress.\n- Counterexamples: Newcomb (source says philosophers disagree), Death in Damascus, adversarial offer, psychopath. Need discuss source claims.\n- My operational view limitations: do-model assumes causal graph known; in predictor problems, the agent's choice may be correlated with predictor through same algorithm; if model includes decision algorithm as common cause, one-shot intervention may still not satisfy \"sane\" because CDT insists not to use action as evidence. There is no universally accepted CDT answer. Possible responses: ratifiability, policy evaluation, update on dispositions. Need mark as proposals.\n\nNeed maybe explain Death in Damascus in more detail:\n\"You are in Damascus, Death appears, says appointment tomorrow in Aleppo? You can flee to Aleppo or stay. Death predicts where you will be and goes there. If reliable, whichever you choose is evidence Death is there. CDT: your current choice does not cause Death's location (already predicted), so no causal benefit; may be indifferent or choose based on tiny travel cost. But intuitive rationality may prefer randomization? Egan says CDT unable to process correlation.\" Need mention.\n\nNeed mention diachronic Dutch books: Oesterheld & Conitzer argue CDT agents can be made to accept sequences of bets losing money. Source. Need maybe not deep.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"statistical independence of A and A→O_j\" from source. Could include in dependencies section: \"If the act is independent of the background variables that make counterfactual true, then the counterfactual probability equals conditional probability. Formally, if P(A □→ O | A)=P(A □→ O) and certain conditional axioms hold, then P(A □→ O)=P(O|A). In randomized trials, this holds.\" Need be cautious. Source says Gibbard/Harper showed if accept two axioms, statistical independence of A and A→O_j suffices to guarantee P(A→O_j)=P(O_j|A). We can quote. Need maybe explain with common cause absent. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"principle of conditional excluded middle\" controversial. Source. Need not dwell.\n\nNeed maybe include \"counterfactual probability of it being the case that A necessarily implies O_j\" maybe weird. Could reinterpret as \"probability that the counterfactual 'if A were taken, O_j would result' is true\". Need note source's wording. Good.\n\nNeed ensure not too many citations. References at end:\n1. Gibbard & Harper 1981.\n2. Lewis 1981.\n3. Lewis 1976.\n4. Egan 2007.\n5. Pearl 2009 (Causality) - additional.\n6. Wikipedia source. Maybe also Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021, Joyce 2012? But references maybe not lengthy. Need maybe include all cited. Target includes references. Could list 8.\n\nNeed check word count after drafting. Let's write in final. But before final, maybe analyze word count. I'll draft in my head with approximate count. Need maybe produce around 2600 words. Use headings and paragraphs. Let's start writing final directly but careful.\n\nPotential final:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Expected Utility Under Intervention\n\nCausal decision theory (CDT) is a family of decision rules... According to the reference article, CDT says rational agent should select action that causes best outcome in expectation; contrasts with EDT... etc.\n\nNeed maybe include \"The reference article leaves open how to compute counterfactual probabilities; much of the operational machinery below is my own.\" Good.\n\nLet's formulate.\n\nDetailed draft:\n\n---\n\nCausal decision theory (CDT) is best understood as the view that choices should be assessed by the expected value of what they would bring about, not by the evidential news they provide. If eating an apple tends to cause happiness and eating an orange causes sadness, CDT recommends the apple. If the apple may be bad, the recommendation depends on expected causal consequences: prior probability it is good, utility of happiness, disutility of sadness. The source article presents this informal idea and the Gibbard-Harper formula. It also notes CDT's contrast with evidential decision theory (EDT), its reliance on counterfactual probabilities, and several objections. The source does not give a complete algorithm for computing the relevant probabilities. In this essay I reconstruct CDT formally, first with counterfactuals and then—my own operational proposal—with causal interventions.\n\nNeed count ~170.\n\nSection 1:\n\"Let A be act variable...\" etc.\n\nNeed include equations.\n\nMaybe use \\square\\to for counterfactual. Need ensure readable.\n\nWrite:\nFor finite outcomes O_j, EDT:\n\\[ V_{EDT}(a)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A=a)D(O_j). \\]\nCDT:\n\\[ V_{CDT}(a)=\\sum_j P(a \\square\\to O_j)D(O_j). \\]\nThe source uses P(A→O_j). D is desirability. The arrow is counterfactual. It asks: how probable is it that if I were to do a, O_j would obtain? Not: how probable is O_j given that I do a? Lewis proved probability of conditional generally not conditional probability. If equal, CDT collapses to EDT.\n\nNeed maybe mention when equal under independence.\n\nSection 2:\nImaging:\nPossible worlds W, prior P. For each w, let w_a be closest A-world. Then P(a □→ O)=∑_w P(w)1_{O}(w_a). If stochastic, kernel. Source says this requires beliefs under certainty of A and may regress. Good.\n\nThen SCM:\nM=(U,V,F,P(U)). For each endogenous variable V_i=F_i(PA_i,U_i). Intervention do(A=a) replaces F_A by constant. Let O_a(u) be value of O in manipulated model with exogenous U=u. Then:\n\\[V_{CDT}(a)=\\sum_u P(u)D(O_a(u))\\]\nor integral. If evidence e observed before choice:\n\\[V_{CDT}(a\\mid e)=\\sum_u P(u\\mid e)D(O_a(u)) = \\sum_o D(o)P(o\\mid do(a),e).\\]\nThis is my proposal. It captures counterfactuals by holding exogenous background fixed while changing action. It distinguishes causes from evidence.\n\nNeed maybe mention model uncertainty:\nIf H hypotheses with prior P(h):\n\\[V_{CDT}(a)=\\sum_h P(h)\\sum_o D(o)P_h(o\\mid do(a)).\\]\nSource's discussion of dependency hypotheses fits this. Egan criticizes holding fixed unconditional credences. We can update P(h|e) with non-action evidence; but CDT does not replace P(h) by P(h|a). Need mark.\n\nSection 3 recipe:\n- Step 1: specify decision problem.\n- Step 2: causal graph.\n- Step 3: compute interventional distribution. Use adjustment: if Z backdoor satisfies, P(o|do(a))=∑_zP(o|a,z)P(z). If no confounding, equals conditional. If front-door, etc. If impossible, use simulation/structural equations.\n- Step 4: maximize. For policies: π maps histories to actions; evaluate V(π)=E_{do(π)}[D(O)]. In sequential setting, do(π) sets each action variable to π_t of prior variables. Observations remain generated by model. This treats feedback properly.\n\nNeed maybe include \"relevant dependencies\" in this section: CDT keeps causal dependencies downstream of action; severs incoming arrows to action. Common causes remain. Colliders etc. Need maybe write.\n\nSection examples:\nApple: p good. U_apple=pD(H)+(1-p)D(S). If orange known sad U_orange=D(S_o). No confounding, EDT=CDT.\n\nDavid: C charisma, A summon, R revolt. C affects R and disposition to A; A does not affect C; unjust acts don't cause R. Let q=P(C=ch), r_c, r_u revolt probabilities, b benefit of Bathsheba, c cost revolt. CDT's causal probability of revolt under do(summon) is q r_c+(1-q)r_u (assuming summoning itself doesn't change revolt risk). EDT uses P(C|A=summon), overweighting uncharismatic. Thus EDT may abstain; CDT may summon if b>c*baseline risk. Source says CDT recommends sending; this illustrates treating charisma as fixed background. Need maybe mention if David has evidence about charisma, both update prior.\n\nNewcomb: predictor, boxes. Let q prior probability predictor predicted one-box. Contents already set. Under do(one): V= q*1M+(1-q)*0. Under do(two): q*1,001,000+(1-q)*1,000. Difference = 1,000, so two-box. This is not because CDT thinks two-boxing is evidence of empty box; it ignores that evidence. EDT with 99% accuracy yields one-box: V_EDT(one)≈0.99*1M, V_EDT(two)≈0.99*1000+0.01*1,001,000 ≈ 10,990. Source's claim. Need note philosophers disagree.\n\nPsychopath button: variable P. Press B. If B causes elimination of psychopaths; if Paul is psychopath, pressing causes his death. Paul believes only psychopath would press, so P(P|B=press) high. CDT: do(B=press) holds P fixed at prior/admissible posterior. If prior P(P) low and world without psychopaths valuable, V_CDT(press) may exceed not pressing. EDT conditions on B and recommends not. Source/Egan says CDT endorses pressing, widely counterintuitive. Need mention Joyce response: if Paul has actual evidence of psychopathic inclinations, CDT should condition on that evidence before intervention. But counterexample relies on act itself being evidence.\n\nMaybe include Adversarial Offer in criticism: Source says CDT buys box because causal expected value >=1.50. Need explain as adversarial prediction. Could include short paragraph: \"Similarly, Adversarial Offer...\" Need maybe word count.\n\nSection Dependencies and agreement:\nNeed maybe earlier? Could have a subsection after examples: \"What counts as a relevant dependency?\" Explain CDT doesn't ignore all correlations; it ignores correlations that are not causal pathways from action to outcome, but it uses correlations that are consequences of action or mediated by action. If action causes a symptom that predicts outcome, symptom is downstream; intervention includes it. If common cause, no. If action changes a variable that then changes outcome, include. If policy uses observation, observation is not intervened upon; it's part of causal process. Good.\n\nCould include in recipe.\n\nCriticism/open questions:\n- Vagueness/imaging regress source.\n- Counterexamples. Death in Damascus: need explain. CDT may not know to flee? If Death predicts, action doesn't cause Death's location. If staying has no travel cost and fleeing maybe cost, CDT might stay, but if Death reliable and predicted flee, staying leads death? Wait scenario: Death says \"I have appointment with you tomorrow in Damascus\" maybe? Let's recall classic: You are in Damascus, Death appears, you flee to Aleppo, Death says \"I have appointment with you tomorrow in Aleppo\"? Actually story: Death in Damascus: A man sees Death in Damascus, flees to Aleppo to avoid Death, but Death predicted he would flee to Aleppo and is waiting there; if he had stayed, Death would have been in Damascus? The rational action unc